---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Methods - Recovery Observatory">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Methods</p>
      <h1>From affected boundary to recovery trajectory.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The observatory now treats the disaster region as the unit of analysis:
        define the boundary, attach administrative units, aggregate indicators,
        and keep uncertainty visible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid two">
        <article class="card"><h3>Spatial Frame</h3><p>Start with a verified affected-area boundary, then connect relevant administrative units and comparable grids or zones.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Temporal Frame</h3><p>Store annual or milestone observations to distinguish response, reconstruction, stabilization, and long-run transformation.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Indicator Harmonization</h3><p>Aggregate values inside the disaster boundary using consistent units, time windows, spatial resolution, and uncertainty notes.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Mixed Evidence</h3><p>Combine remote sensing, settlement data, local statistics, policy texts, field observations, interviews, and event-related social sensing.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Reproducible Pipeline</p>
          <h2>Every value needs a source trail.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          Fetch scripts write provenance logs first. Processing then turns event
          and affected-area inputs into chart-ready files. Missing geospatial
          layers stay marked as <code>manual_required</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid three">
        <article class="card"><h3>Fetch</h3><p><code>scripts/fetch_usgs.py</code> and <code>fetch_reliefweb.py</code> collect event metadata and report records under <code>public/data/provenance/</code>.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Process</h3><p><code>scripts/process_all.py</code> standardizes earthquake metadata, report frequency, and documented affected-area data gaps into <code>public/data/processed/</code>.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Review</h3><p>Remote-sensing and settlement indicators are published only after boundary selection, raster processing, masking, and zonal statistics are complete.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
